= Introduction <sec:intro>

== Motivation <sec:intro-motivation>

*Warehouse fleets jam.* Warehouse robots share narrow aisles, intersections and pick stations. Even the planning literature, which treats the warehouse as a stream of path finding problems, treats congestion as first-order: routing each robot along its free-flow shortest path creates congestion @chen2023traffic, and a solver that plans over a bounded horizon can leave two agents in a corridor waiting for each other @li2020lifelong. When robots meet head-on in an aisle, or several reach a junction together, the fleet can stop, and the deadlock does not dissolve on its own: each robot holds the floor it stands on while it waits for floor held by another, the structure of the classical "deadly embrace" @coffman1971system.

*Local reactive control cannot break the symmetry.* Many robots navigate with a local stack such as Nav2 @macenski2020marathon, which plans for one robot at a time and treats other robots as obstacles. Reactive methods decide how to avoid a collision, not who should give way. A planner can conclude that every path forward is unsafe and stop although a feasible path exists @trautman2010unfreezing, and for controllers built on control barrier functions deadlock is an equilibrium on the boundary of the safe set @grover2019deadlock. Two robots that face each other and run the same recovery policy can recreate the stall they are trying to escape (@sec:bg-reactive).

*A slow semantic layer could supply what is missing.* The proposed remedies for reactive deadlock share one structure: a slower layer sets an order or a timing, and a faster layer keeps the motion safe (@sec:bg-reactive). A fixed overhead camera gives the slow layer a view of the whole situation, and vision-language models (VLMs), which read an image with a text description and answer in a structured form, can turn that view into a decision: name the kind of jam and choose a way out from a closed vocabulary of strategies. Closely related work already calls a VLM on a stall, shows it a top-view image and uses it as a high-level planner above a safe low-level controller @garg2024foundation @garg2026online.

*A model's plan cannot be trusted as it stands.* Language models do not plan reliably on their own @valmeekam2023planning, and the emerging position is to treat their output as a candidate that external, model-based checks must verify @kambhampati2024llms. A VLM is also slow, costly and fallible: it may time out, refuse, return malformed output, or name a yielder with no room to back up. The design question is therefore also how its decision becomes safe commands, and how much of the executed plan can still be credited to the model.

== Problem Statement <sec:intro-problem>

This report studies a hybrid, two-layer coordinator for a warehouse robot fleet. *Local navigation* (Layer 1) is fast and per robot: each robot runs its own Nav2 stack, with a design target of 20–50 Hz #ev("1.01"). The *coordination layer* (Layer 2) is the slow, centralised loop, with a design target of 0.5–2 Hz #ev("1.01"), that detects a stall, asks the VLM, grounds the decision and dispatches commands; with the *coordination layer off*, only each robot's local navigation runs. When the stall detector fires, the orchestrator sends the annotated overhead frame and the tracked robot states to the VLM, which classifies the congestion and chooses one of eight strategies #ev("1.02"). The *grounder*, a deterministic, geometry-driven layer, turns the decision into commands: it checks feasibility, repairs or replaces an infeasible plan, and refuses when no safe manoeuvre exists. Only the yielder's retreat room is checked against static obstacles; escort segments and reverse legs are not (@sec:design-grounding). An independent safety monitor enforces robot-to-robot distance and speed limits on every command, with one exemption for clearing manoeuvres (@sec:design-safety).

One property shapes every claim in the report: the robots always execute the grounder's output, except that an opt-in switch, used only in the demonstration campaign, lets a verified model plan set the order of the back-out phases. Each dispatch carries a *plan-audit label*: the model's plan was *accepted unchanged*, *repaired*, or replaced by the *grounder default*. The label records how the proposal fared in the audit, not which commands came from it. The intersection run 1 registration states that every plan in an earlier live intersection pilot was repaired or replaced #ev("1.13"), true of live ER-2 plans (0 of 20 accepted unchanged), not of the October gpt-4o pilot (11 of 13) #ev("6.02"). Claims are therefore made about the pipeline, and the model's share is measured apart.

The problem addressed is the following. _Can a pipeline in which a stall trigger detects congestion, a VLM given the annotated overhead frame and a text list of robot states confirms and classifies it, and a deterministic grounder turns its decision into commands checked against robot-pair safety limits, clear congestion that local reactive control leaves in place, and what does each part contribute?_ It is posed for a small simulated fleet and answered with one registered pilot, exploratory runs and post hoc decision-level analyses; any success is credited to the pipeline, not to the model alone. The project proposal framed a comparison against classical geometric coordinators; the experiments instead compare the pipeline with the coordination layer off on the same code, and with a scripted arm that skips the VLM call and runs the grounder's default plan (@sec:intro-scope).

== Research Questions <sec:intro-rq>

Three research questions split the problem by stage:

- *RQ1, perception and classification.* Does the VLM classify the congestion type and the kind of blocker correctly, and tell congestion apart from normal flow? Because the VLM is called only after the stall trigger fires, RQ1 concerns post-trigger classification and false positives, not open-world detection.
- *RQ2, strategy competence.* Do the strategies the VLM chooses clear congestion?
- *RQ3, social reasoning.* Are the robot the VLM names to yield, and the crossing order it proposes, effective?

*Scope change.* The first registered design (tagged 30 September 2026) listed these and two further questions as a skeleton for this report, each with a planned test #ev("1.03"): whether the VLM uses information no classical baseline has, such as what a blocker is, and whether feedback about a failed command changes its next decision. No confirmatory data were collected under that design, and no later design tested the two. They were not tested in this work: no registered or controlled comparison addressed them. Exploratory runs before that design was tagged touched both, with first blocker-kind answers in a funded battery and two gpt-4o recovery runs on a blocked head-on scene #ev("1.16")\; they are not reported as evidence here. Both questions were moved to Future Work (@sec:concl-future) when this report was finalised; the narrowing was made after the experiments, is part of no registered design, and changes no result.

The studies answer narrower questions, stated per study in @sec:ch5-rq; @tab:intro-rq-map maps each to the questions it informs, and none is pooled with another.

